---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import {
  ACTIVITY_IDS,
  ACTIVITY_PRESETS,
  FLAG_THRESHOLDS,
  RAIN_DIVISORS,
  SCORING_VERSION,
  WIND_THRESHOLDS,
} from '../domain/activities';
import { FLAG_COPY } from '../domain/flags';

const rainLabel = { avoid: 'Avoid rain', light: 'Some rain is fine', okay: "Rain doesn't bother me" } as const;
const windLabel = { low: 'Prefer calm', medium: 'Some wind is fine', high: "Wind doesn't bother me" } as const;
const pct = (n: number) => `${Math.round(n * 100)}%`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology — Outside?"
  description="How Outside? turns a forecast into a preference match: inputs, presets, formulas, flags and limitations."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How Outside? works</h1>
    <p>
      Outside? compares the forecast with the preferences for the activity you choose. The result is a
      <strong>preference match</strong>, not a safety assessment, a medical judgment or an official warning. The
      thresholds below are product defaults chosen for this tool, not researched comfort or health limits. Scoring
      version {SCORING_VERSION}.
    </p>

    <h2>What it looks at</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Feels-like (apparent) temperature, from the forecast.</li>
      <li>Chance of precipitation and precipitation amount for each hour.</li>
      <li>Sustained wind and gusts.</li>
      <li>Weather type (for example rain, snow, fog or thunderstorms) and whether it is daylight.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      It does <strong>not</strong> check air quality, UV, pollen, official weather alerts, visibility detail, terrain,
      trail or road surfaces, or water conditions. A good match says nothing about those.
    </p>

    <h2>Activity presets</h2>
    <p>You can change each activity's comfortable range and its rain and wind tolerance under Preferences.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Activity</th>
            <th scope="col">Comfortable feels-like</th>
            <th scope="col">Temperature weight</th>
            <th scope="col">Rain weight</th>
            <th scope="col">Wind weight</th>
            <th scope="col">Default rain</th>
            <th scope="col">Default wind</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            ACTIVITY_IDS.map((id) => {
              const p = ACTIVITY_PRESETS[id];
              return (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{p.label}</th>
                  <td>
                    {p.defaults.comfortLowC} to {p.defaults.comfortHighC} °C
                  </td>
                  <td>{pct(p.weights.temperature)}</td>
                  <td>{pct(p.weights.precipitation)}</td>
                  <td>{pct(p.weights.wind)}</td>
                  <td>{rainLabel[p.defaults.rain]}</td>
                  <td>{windLabel[p.defaults.wind]}</td>
                </tr>
              );
            })
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>“Outdoors” is meant for casual, land-based time outside. It is not a model for swimming, climbing, boating or remote hiking.</p>

    <h2>How an hour is scored</h2>
    <p>Each hour gets three penalties between 0 and 1:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Temperature:</strong> 0 inside your comfortable range, rising to 1 when the feels-like temperature is 12 °C
        outside it. Humidity is already part of feels-like, so it is shown but not counted again. Wind is part of
        feels-like too, and is also counted on its own because it affects effort, especially on a bike.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Rain:</strong> the larger of the chance of precipitation and the amount, each compared with your tolerance:
        {
          (Object.keys(RAIN_DIVISORS) as (keyof typeof RAIN_DIVISORS)[]).map((k, i) => (
            <>
              {i > 0 && '; '}
              {rainLabel[k].toLowerCase()} reaches 1 at {RAIN_DIVISORS[k].probabilityPct}% or {RAIN_DIVISORS[k].amountMm} mm per hour
            </>
          ))
        }.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Wind:</strong> how far sustained wind and gusts exceed your tolerance (
        {
          (Object.keys(WIND_THRESHOLDS) as (keyof typeof WIND_THRESHOLDS)[]).map((k, i) => (
            <>
              {i > 0 && '; '}
              {windLabel[k].toLowerCase()}: from {WIND_THRESHOLDS[k].sustainedKmh} km/h, gusts from {WIND_THRESHOLDS[k].gustKmh} km/h
            </>
          ))
        }).
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The score is 100 minus the weighted penalties. If any single penalty is high, the score is also capped so that one
      unacceptable factor cannot be averaged away by good ones: a fully unacceptable factor always gives a poor match.
      Scores of 75 or more are a <strong>Good match</strong>, 45 to 75 <strong>Mixed conditions</strong>, and below 45 a
      <strong>Poor match</strong>. If any needed value is missing, the hour shows <strong>Limited data</strong> instead
      of a guess.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reasons name a factor only when it is noticeable, so a 5% chance of rain is not described as “higher chance of
      precipitation”.
    </p>

    <h2>Weather that is always flagged</h2>
    <p>
      These are shown whenever they appear in the forecast, whatever your preferences, and those hours are never
      suggested. They are conservative app rules, not official hazard thresholds, and no flag does not mean no hazard.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>{FLAG_COPY.thunderstorm}</li>
      <li>{FLAG_COPY.freezing_precip}</li>
      <li>{FLAG_COPY.strong_gusts} (gusts of {FLAG_THRESHOLDS.strongGustKmh} km/h or more)</li>
      <li>{FLAG_COPY.heavy_precip} ({FLAG_THRESHOLDS.heavyPrecipMm} mm or more in an hour)</li>
      <li>{FLAG_COPY.high_heat} (feels like {FLAG_THRESHOLDS.highHeatFeelsLikeC} °C or more)</li>
      <li>{FLAG_COPY.severe_cold} (feels like {FLAG_THRESHOLDS.severeColdFeelsLikeC} °C or less)</li>
      <li>{FLAG_COPY.snow_or_ice_concern}</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Fog is shown as a note but does not by itself exclude an hour.</p>

    <h2>Best times</h2>
    <p>
      For the length you choose (30 minutes to 2 hours), Outside? checks every start time in the next 24 hours: now and
      each following hour. A window's score combines the average of its hours (70%) with its worst hour (30%), so one bad
      hour counts against a longer outing. Windows with missing data, flagged weather, a poor hour or night hours (when
      “Daylight only” is on) are not suggested. Up to three non-overlapping windows are shown, best first; ties go to the
      earlier time. Poor windows are never added just to fill the list.
    </p>

    <h2>Hourly approximations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Forecasts are hourly. Temperature, humidity and sustained wind are taken at the start of each hour; precipitation,
        its chance and gusts describe the hour that follows. A 30-minute outing uses the whole hour's forecast, and
        precipitation is never scaled down for a shorter outing.
      </li>
      <li>An hour counts as daylight only if it is daylight at both its start and its end, so dawn and dusk hours are left out of daylight-only suggestions.</li>
      <li>“Now” comes from the provider's current conditions and is kept separate from the next-hour match.</li>
      <li>Times are shown in the selected place's time zone, including across daylight-saving changes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Freshness</h2>
    <p>
      A forecast is refreshed after 15 minutes. If it can't be refreshed, it stays visible; after an hour suggestions are
      paused and the forecast is marked as earlier, and after two hours it is no longer shown.
    </p>

    <h2>Data sources</h2>
    <p>
      Weather data by <a href="https://open-meteo.com/">Open-Meteo</a>, licensed under{' '}
      <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>. Location search uses data from{' '}
      <a href="https://www.geonames.org/">GeoNames</a> via Open-Meteo. Forecasts are model output for a grid area, not
      street-level observations, and can be wrong.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
